---
name: try-catch-exception-delivery
description: try/catch actually delivers a thrown exception to the handler, matched by supertype (Throwable/Exception/RuntimeException) or a wildcard
backends: [int, jvm, js]
---

# `try` / `catch` — the exception actually reaches the handler

`BUGS.md` `v2-native-front-try-catch` layer 2. The parse half of that bug had been fixed; what
remained is that on the v2 lanes a **thrown** exception never reached the handler. A host error is
delivered as `DataV("RuntimeException", [message])` while `catch { case e: Throwable => … }` lowers
to a nominal tag test `__isTag__(v, "Throwable", -1)` — and the tag test was flat, with no notion of
supertypes, so it failed and the handler fell through to `match: no matching case`.

Every case below therefore has to actually **throw**: a `try` whose body never fails passes even
with the bug present, which is exactly why the bug survived (`try { println(x) } catch …` worked).
The v1 reference rule this pins is `PatternRuntime.isExceptionSupertype` — a pattern naming
`Throwable` / `Exception` / `RuntimeException` / `Error` matches the synthesized exception, because
a `catch` scrutinee carries the throwable's simple name and users catch it by supertype.

`js` is back in the backend list. It was excluded because `10 / 0` evaluated to `Infinity` there, so
no `catch` was ever entered — the divergence this case surfaced and that was filed rather than swept
under the restriction (`BUGS.md` `js-int-division-by-zero-yields-infinity`). With that fixed, the JS
lane throws like the others and belongs here.

The **braceless multiline** shape is in as well, now that it works. It is a load-bearing case rather
than a decorative one: its failure mode was to swallow the rest of the file, so the `println`s that
follow it are what detect a regression — the case would print *less*, not fail an assertion.

```scalascript
def divide(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case e: Throwable => "caught-throwable" }

def viaException(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case e: Exception => "caught-exception" }

def viaRuntime(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case e: RuntimeException => "caught-runtime" }

// An UNTYPED arm is a catch-all. Both shapes are here because F's handler-arm parser used to assume
// every arm was `pat : T =>` and read the arm's BODY as the type name — which made the tag test
// unsatisfiable and, on the default lane, silently dropped the whole program at exit 0.
def viaWildcard(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case _ => "caught-wildcard" }

def viaBinder(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case e => "caught-binder" }

// BRACELESS multiline, as a def body. This shape used to leave the def's block open — F's handler-arm
// parser ate the VIRTUAL `}` that layout had inserted to close the def body — so everything after it
// was swallowed into the def and the program printed nothing at exit 0. The `println`s below are
// therefore part of the assertion: if the block reopened, they would vanish rather than fail.
def viaBraceless(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try
    (a / b).toString
  catch
    case e: Throwable => "caught-braceless"

// The handler must NOT fire when nothing is thrown — otherwise a `catch` that swallowed every
// successful result would also satisfy the four assertions above.
println(divide(10, 0))
println(viaException(10, 0))
println(viaRuntime(10, 0))
println(viaWildcard(10, 0))
println(viaBinder(10, 0))
println(viaBraceless(10, 0))
println(divide(10, 2))
println(viaRuntime(9, 3))
println(viaWildcard(8, 4))
```
